Essentially, when you're using generative answers, you're effectively answering what does our content say about this?

572
00:48:58,672 --> 00:49:03,072
As in, what does these knowledge sources say about this user's prompt?

573
00:49:03,232 --> 00:49:07,312
In Copilot Studio, there's a bunch of different supported knowledge sources in particular.

574
00:49:07,598 --> 00:49:11,712
Microsoft Learn content for the exam covered these sort of things.

575
00:49:11,872 --> 00:49:16,832
We can have public websites, we can upload documents directly to our agent.

576
00:49:16,912 --> 00:49:24,512
And when you upload that, if you've ever built a copilot studio agent, it actually takes that information, chunks it, and indexes it inside of Dataverse.

577
00:49:24,672 --> 00:49:33,380
And so what that means is that's actually where your agent is going and getting that information technically, which I'll be honest was something I wasn't necessarily aware of or where that was indexed.

578
00:49:33,472 --> 00:49:37,312
But if you've ever uploaded a document, sometimes it can take like 10 minutes and just sit there processing.

579
00:49:37,472 --> 00:49:41,872
The reason is because it's chunking and indexing that information for your agent to be able to call on.

580
00:49:41,952 --> 00:49:47,792
We can add SharePoint knowledge sources, we can add Dataverse tables, we can also use enterprise connectors like OneDrive.

581
00:49:47,872 --> 00:49:51,472
We can also use Azure OpenAI connected data and Azure AI Search.

582
00:49:51,566 --> 00:49:56,592
And we're gonna talk about Azure AI Search more here in just a moment, but there is a limitation.

583
00:49:56,752 --> 00:50:00,912
You can have a total of 500 knowledge sources on an agent, which is a lot.

584
00:50:00,992 --> 00:50:04,032
I've never seen anybody even come close to needing to use that.

585
00:50:04,272 --> 00:50:07,552
You can only have what are called five unstructured knowledge sources.

586
00:50:07,712 --> 00:50:15,072
An unstructured knowledge source would be like a document, it would be like a public website, it would be like a SharePoint document or a SharePoint folder, right?

587
00:50:15,152 --> 00:50:16,432
This is unstructured.

588
00:50:16,672 --> 00:50:18,912
Structured is like tabular data, right?

589
00:50:19,054 --> 00:50:25,792
So you could add like 500 different Dataverse tables to your agent, but you couldn't add 500 documents to your agent.

590
00:50:26,352 --> 00:50:32,352
Generative answers allows you to call specific knowledge at specific times for your agent.

591
00:50:32,558 --> 00:50:33,632
All right, that is the concept.

592
00:50:33,742 --> 00:50:41,552
And when we talk about Azure AI Search, for the sake of simplicity, think of Azure AI Search as the Prograde Knowledge Retrieval tool.

593
00:50:41,712 --> 00:50:41,952
All right.

594
00:50:42,032 --> 00:50:49,952
So if you, a lot of people want to connect their agent to a PDF document, that is large, or a folder of PDF documents that has 10 of them.

595
00:50:50,032 --> 00:51:00,032
and they're all 200 pages long and all sorts of this information, that is outside of the realm of what's possible with a Copilot Studio agent, where you have this tool called Azure AI Search.

596
00:51:00,112 --> 00:51:02,752
and you can connect your Copilot Studio agents to Azure AI Search.

597
00:51:02,832 --> 00:51:07,952
But Azure AI Search is essentially an index of chunked information.

598
00:51:08,174 --> 00:51:21,312
So instead of say giving your agent an entire PDF document that's got a thousand paragraphs, Azure AI Search will essentially create an item in this repository for every paragraph, so to speak, every chunk.

599
00:51:21,460 --> 00:51:25,260
It's going to create documents they're called or chunks based off of this stuff.

600
00:51:25,340 --> 00:51:29,980
And what happens is this Azure AI search actually has like an LLM baked into it.

601
00:51:30,032 --> 00:51:35,792
And so you can essentially query specific chunks just searching the Azure AI search.

602
00:51:35,872 --> 00:51:39,232
And so what this means is, if you're if you've lost me, trust, I got you.

603
00:51:39,392 --> 00:51:44,592
What this means is your agent, instead of just calling the document directly, will call Azure AI Search.

604
00:51:44,718 --> 00:51:47,312
And say I say, tell me about our phishing policy.

605
00:51:47,472 --> 00:52:01,472
Instead of looking at a massive document that covers our phishing policy and our scam policy and our email policy and our login policy and password reset processes and our benefits, instead of one document containing all of that information, instead I call the Azure AI Search.

606
00:52:01,552 --> 00:52:04,112
The agent then uses Azure AI Search to search for phishing.

607
00:52:04,272 --> 00:52:09,792
Azure AI search then retrieves the handful of chunks or documents that actually apply to phishing.

608
00:52:09,998 --> 00:52:14,592
It condenses the information that's actually available to our agent to be able to use.

609
00:52:14,752 --> 00:52:15,552
That's the idea.

610
00:52:15,632 --> 00:52:18,432
And so you need to be aware of chunking chunk sizes.

611
00:52:18,574 --> 00:52:22,832
Chunk size is essentially each document in that thing, how much text is inside of it.

612
00:52:22,912 --> 00:52:25,792
You can have what's called keyword or semantic search.

613
00:52:25,952 --> 00:52:32,032
Keyword search is essentially, you know, if I type in phishing into Azure AI search, it's going to return all the documents with the word phishing.

614
00:52:32,192 --> 00:52:39,552
But semantic could be, you know, it's going to bring back anything that could even be related to phishing that might not actually use that word, right?

615
00:52:39,632 --> 00:52:44,352
Like scams or suspicious links or threats or something like that, right?

616
00:52:44,432 --> 00:52:46,352
This idea that's semantic search.

617
00:52:46,478 --> 00:52:50,272
And the field schema is important for you to be aware of as well in terms of Azure AI search.

618
00:52:50,382 --> 00:52:56,032
This is how you determine on each of those documents what can actually be searched by the Azure AI search tool.

619
00:52:56,174 --> 00:53:00,672
And again, this is a uh a knowledge type tool you can add to your agent.

620
00:53:00,912 --> 00:53:03,632
We continue on and talk about tools.

621
00:53:03,792 --> 00:53:11,872
This is almost the last piece of what we talk about in terms of agent fundamentals, but I mentioned this is this is one of our longer ones.

622
00:53:12,032 --> 00:53:16,032
Agent tools are the thing that actually give our agent the ability to do things.

623
00:53:16,206 --> 00:53:23,712
So we have things like connectors, we have custom connectors, we have MCP tools, we have agent flows, we have REST APIs and HTTP actions.

624
00:53:23,872 --> 00:53:27,552
We also have this thing called agent-to-agent protocol or agent orchestration.

625
00:53:27,726 --> 00:53:29,952
And I want to walk through each one of these very briefly.

626
00:53:30,112 --> 00:53:33,232
Again, as an architect, if you're feeling overwhelmed, it's okay.

627
00:53:33,312 --> 00:53:40,912
You don't need to know all of the nuances of how these specifically work or have even built all of these, but you just need to know that these things exist.

628
00:53:41,152 --> 00:53:46,660
You just need to know that these things exist and that you have the ability to write these in your blueprint.

629
00:53:46,832 --> 00:53:50,912
In your business use case, you have the ability to say, hey, let's leverage this.

630
00:53:51,118 --> 00:53:54,432
Let's build a Copile Studio agent that uses an MCP server.

631
00:53:54,512 --> 00:53:57,472
You need to know the scenario on why you should do that.

632
00:53:57,632 --> 00:53:59,232
So let's talk about connectors.

633
00:53:59,392 --> 00:54:02,992
Again, connectors are, you know, how we talk to other services.

634
00:54:03,072 --> 00:54:17,632
So if you want an agent that connects to Outlook or connects to Dataverse or connects to SharePoint or connects to ServiceNow or Zendesk or MailChimp or Google Drive or Outlook Calendar or OneNote, you need to connect to that service to give your agent the ability to actually talk to that service.

635
00:54:17,838 --> 00:54:23,952
Connectors are pre-built APIs, and you can even create custom connectors if there's not one that is already pre-built for you.

636
00:54:24,174 --> 00:54:28,432
The pre-built ones are kind of created and managed by Microsoft inside of the Power Platform.

637
00:54:28,592 --> 00:54:33,152
But you can also create your own custom connectors as well if there's not one supported there for you.

638
00:54:33,312 --> 00:54:42,212
There are hundreds or there's thousands of actions, hundreds of connectors available to all sorts of different third party services, including Microsoft and outside of Microsoft.

639
00:54:43,118 --> 00:54:46,240
ones the next thing that's important to talk about is model context protocol.

640
00:54:46,400 --> 00:54:51,040
Model context protocol is something that can feel very confusing for a lot of people.

641
00:54:51,200 --> 00:54:57,520
Really, what model context protocol is, is a layer between the actual tool and the agent.

642
00:54:57,646 --> 00:55:02,720
And so what I mean by that is when we talk about connectors, we'll use the send an email concept, right?

643
00:55:02,800 --> 00:55:05,028
Let's say we want to build an agent that sends an email.

644
00:55:05,200 --> 00:55:10,720
There is a connector, an action using the outlook connector in the power platform that is send an email.

645
00:55:10,800 --> 00:55:14,320
If you're familiar with Power Automate, it's that same connector, it's the same action.

646
00:55:14,414 --> 00:55:17,040
And so we can build an agent that uses that tool.

647
00:55:17,120 --> 00:55:19,360
So we can add that tool directly to our agent.

648
00:55:19,520 --> 00:55:24,160
Well, this concept of MCP is actually putting a layer between those two.

649
00:55:24,320 --> 00:55:38,880
And so what I mean by that is instead of connecting the send an email action or tool directly to our agent, there could be a Outlook MCP, which there probably is an Outlook MCP that has a host of different tools.

650
00:55:39,054 --> 00:55:41,600
And we connect that MCP to our agent.

651
00:55:41,774 --> 00:55:43,200
Now if you're like why though?

652
00:55:43,360 --> 00:55:45,280
Like why not just connect it directly?

653
00:55:45,440 --> 00:55:52,996
Why MCP is because this helps us can help us ensure consistent business semantics across AI agents.

654
00:55:53,200 --> 00:56:00,800
And so really what this means is this allows us to have multi-app interoperability and shared enterprise logic.

655
00:56:00,974 --> 00:56:02,480
So specifically what do I mean?

656
00:56:02,640 --> 00:56:11,120
When we are leveraging an MCP server or model context protocol, let's say that we want to build a bunch of agents that connect to Outlook.

657
00:56:11,200 --> 00:56:15,760
And all of these agents are going to send an email, draft an email, and delete an email, right?

658
00:56:15,840 --> 00:56:17,440
They all are going to have those tools.

659
00:56:17,600 --> 00:56:26,080
Well if I wanted to build say 10 agents that all do those three things, I would need to add those three tools to all 10 agents.

660
00:56:26,160 --> 00:56:27,492
I would need to do 30 updates.

661
00:56:27,694 --> 00:56:31,360
Like I would need to do that 30 times add tools to our agents.

662
00:56:31,440 --> 00:56:39,440
Where instead if I made a MCP server that had these three tools then all I have to do is add the MCP server to 10 agents.

663
00:56:39,534 --> 00:56:44,480
That's only 10 updates and say what happens if I need to change how it deletes emails.

664
00:56:44,640 --> 00:56:48,880
If I'm using MCP all I have to do is go make one update on the MCP server.

665
00:56:49,040 --> 00:56:56,160
If I connect these tools directly to the agent I need to go and update all 10 agents all of those actions for that all of those agents.

666
00:56:56,334 --> 00:56:59,600
And so it gives us this ability to have interoperability.

667
00:56:59,726 --> 00:57:06,960
We're going to talk more about that concept later but MCP helps us have a controllability layer between the tools and the agent.

668
00:57:07,150 --> 00:57:09,520
so just something to be aware of as we think about that.

669
00:57:09,680 --> 00:57:11,920
We'll talk more about this as well in a later section.

670
00:57:12,080 --> 00:57:13,360
We have agent flows.

671
00:57:13,520 --> 00:57:15,760
Agent flows are power automate flows.

672
00:57:15,840 --> 00:57:21,760
They do have a little bit a little bit different licensing to be aware of I didn't have any kind of licensing questions on my exam.

673
00:57:21,920 --> 00:57:26,240
So for the sake of the exam think of these like power automate flows if you're familiar with the power platform.

674
00:57:26,320 --> 00:57:35,440
Again maybe you're seeing how heavy I feel like this is co-pilot studio because I definitely feel like these were all concepts that you need to know for the exam and I had questions on them.

675
00:57:35,600 --> 00:57:43,120
And so really agent flows are how you can give your indeterministic agent deterministic capabilities.

676
00:57:43,280 --> 00:57:55,920
What I mean by that is say you have a business process, which most organizations' business processes work in this way where you need A to happen, you need like there's step one and then there's step two and then there's step three and then there's step four and then there's step five.

677
00:57:56,000 --> 00:58:17,600
And every single time, those five things need to happen, we should not build an agent that has these five tools and to try to tell the instructions to say, "Call this tool, then this tool, then this tool, then this tool, then this tool .". The reason for that is because Even though we can get really good with our instructions and do all sorts of testing and monitoring, if this is a business process where step one has to happen and then step two has to happen and then step three has to happen.

678
00:58:17,680 --> 00:58:21,280
we should not leverage a large language model to ensure this thing happens.

679
00:58:21,454 --> 00:58:25,920
Because it has its indeterministic technology, it has the ability to hallucinate or not do what we want.

680
00:58:26,000 --> 00:58:41,040
Whereas opposed to an agent flow it is code it's going to do those things every single time and so we should think about leveraging agent flows or workflows to be able to do these deterministic processes and then we can build an agent that just calls this agent flow.

681
00:58:41,198 --> 00:58:47,040
So this agent knows oh wait you want to start step one through five I got you let me call this flow.

682
00:58:47,120 --> 00:58:53,376
That's the idea of when we want to use agent flows prompt actions prompt actions are Pretty interesting.

683
00:58:53,536 --> 00:58:55,856
I had a specific question about prompt actions.

684
00:58:56,046 --> 00:59:07,216
Essentially, what these allow you to do is these allow you to build like little agents, so to speak, little like chat type agents inside of your Power Automate flows or add them to your agent itself.

685
00:59:07,376 --> 00:59:12,016
And so specifically, what this means is the inputs for these prompt actions.

686
00:59:12,096 --> 00:59:16,964
With agents, you can add tools and you can publish them to channels and all sorts of different things.

687
00:59:17,136 --> 00:59:20,816
Prompt actions don't allow you to like publish them to people to talk to.

688
00:59:20,942 --> 00:59:22,016
So they're a little bit different.

689
00:59:22,126 --> 00:59:29,456
But what I mean by they're kind of like little mini agents is you can pass it inputs, you select a model, and there's a series of instructions and it produces an output.

690
00:59:29,536 --> 00:59:29,696
All right.

691
00:59:29,776 --> 00:59:31,204
And it consumes copilot credits.

692
00:59:31,296 --> 00:59:40,336
But say there's a very specific part in your flow where you don't need tools or you don't need like RAG or you don't need the ability to have an agent that could be deployed to a channel.

693
00:59:40,496 --> 00:59:44,816
You just essentially need it to like reason over an input and produce an output.

694
00:59:44,896 --> 00:59:48,016
Let's use a prompt action to achieve those sort of things.

695
00:59:48,176 --> 00:59:53,136
We can also add these to our agents in the old experience, just calling out here right now.

696
00:59:53,326 --> 00:59:58,256
Currently, as of the time of recording, there's no way to add prompt actions to new co-pilot studio agents.

697
00:59:58,382 --> 01:00:01,696
Now, technically, you could add a flow that has prompt actions to them, sure.

698
01:00:01,966 --> 01:00:06,212
In the classic experience, you can add prompt actions directly as tools to your agents.

699
01:00:06,416 --> 01:00:08,416
These are prompt actions and things to be aware of.

700
01:00:08,496 --> 01:00:10,576
There's also this thing called computer use.

701
01:00:10,816 --> 01:00:16,736
Computer use is essentially like giving your agent a computer screen, a mouse, and a keyboard.

702
01:00:16,896 --> 01:00:19,696
And so I mentioned how we have the ability to have custom connectors.

703
01:00:19,918 --> 01:00:24,576
Essentially, what this means is you can build an API to any kind of service that has APIs available.

704
01:00:24,910 --> 01:00:32,976
But say you have a use case where your agent needs to talk to some kind of system that doesn't have APIs available, but they have a website.

705
01:00:33,056 --> 01:00:43,856
And so you can click on this certain place on their website and then click on here and then submit this form or fill this thing out or read this information, but you don't have any way to access it through an API or a connector.

706
01:00:43,936 --> 01:00:45,376
You can leverage computer use.

707
01:00:45,616 --> 01:00:53,296
Computer use enables you to interact with applications and websites just like a human user by using the actual user interface.

708
01:00:53,422 --> 01:00:57,776
It helps you click buttons and links, helps you enter data into forms, helps you navigate web pages.

709
01:00:57,936 --> 01:01:04,416
And while it's doing this, it's using it, it's consuming the information on the screen and using an LLM to understand what should it do next.

710
01:01:04,576 --> 01:01:08,096
Should it click on a button, should it type this key, et cetera, throughout the whole process.

711
01:01:08,256 --> 01:01:10,656
That being said, computer use is pretty expensive.

712
01:01:10,816 --> 01:01:14,016
If there's a connector available, you should default to using that connector.

713
01:01:14,126 --> 01:01:16,484
You should not default, well, we could just use computer use.

714
01:01:16,686 --> 01:01:25,732
Computer use is very expensive because it's basically a live stream of what's happening and constantly the LLM is constantly consuming what is happening on the screen.

715
01:01:25,936 --> 01:01:31,776
And it has the ability to get in circular loops and maybe go back and forth, or if it clicked on the wrong button, now it has to go back.

716
01:01:31,886 --> 01:01:34,096
Like you're consuming credits this entire time.

717
01:01:34,222 --> 01:01:35,696
I'm not saying that you don't use it.

718
01:01:35,822 --> 01:01:41,376
I'm just saying, as an architect, we need to know when we need to put this in our blueprint for our house and all of those different things.

719
01:01:41,550 --> 01:01:44,496
Here's a question that maybe helps hammer home this concept.

720
01:01:44,656 --> 01:01:47,844
Which principle is most important when designing AI agents?

721
01:01:48,142 --> 01:01:52,256
Ensuring the agent has clear intent, guardrails, and scoped permissions.

722
01:01:52,462 --> 01:01:54,416
That one seems pretty good to me.

723
01:01:54,576 --> 01:01:56,656
Adding as many capabilities as possible.

724
01:01:56,878 --> 01:01:58,016
Nope, that is not correct.

725
01:01:58,176 --> 01:02:02,656
We want our agents to be scoped or thin or very specific as possible.

726
01:02:02,830 --> 01:02:09,056
Designing agents without considering data boundaries, let me just tell you, this is never the correct answer.

727
01:02:09,216 --> 01:02:10,416
Actually, the opposite.

728
01:02:10,576 --> 01:02:19,856
If you ever see an answer that's around consider data boundaries or abide by data boundaries, that is the answer, or at least part of the answer.

729
01:02:20,016 --> 01:02:21,936
Removing all user review steps.

730
01:02:22,096 --> 01:02:23,456
Nope, that is not it either.

731
01:02:23,616 --> 01:02:33,216
Also, kind of in the similar vein as this if you see an exam question around human in the loop or human reviews, that is probably a part of the question.

732
01:02:33,390 --> 01:02:35,696
We know agents have the ability to hallucinate.

733
01:02:35,776 --> 01:02:39,456
And so we want to mitigate those risks as much as possible.

734
01:02:39,630 --> 01:02:42,656
And so implementing human review steps is going to be a good thing.

735
01:02:42,766 --> 01:02:43,856
And so, what's the most important?

736
01:02:44,624 --> 01:02:51,664
thing when designing agents, that it has a clear intent, that it's very thin and specific, has a purpose and a scope, that it has guardrails.

737
01:02:51,984 --> 01:02:54,464
What that means is role-based access control.

738
01:02:54,544 --> 01:02:56,464
And we're going to talk about that more in a little bit.

739
01:02:56,624 --> 01:03:05,904
That it has security guardrails in place, that it has authentication set up, that it is safe and secure and doesn't produce harmful content, and that it is scoped permissions.

740
01:03:06,064 --> 01:03:08,516
It doesn't have the ability to do absolutely everything, right?

741
01:03:08,704 --> 01:03:18,384
If we're building an agent that looks at a word document, a chatting agent that looks at a word document and responds, we should not give our agent access to our ERP system for whatever reason.

742
01:03:18,544 --> 01:03:20,384
We should not give it the ability to write emails.

743
01:03:20,544 --> 01:03:23,664
We should not give it the ability to delete tables in Dataverse, right?

744
01:03:23,824 --> 01:03:29,504
That's the idea that the permissions that we add to our agent are scoped and match this clear intent.

745
01:03:29,664 --> 01:03:43,184
Unfortunately, there are a couple of just other agent stuff that the exam covers that didn't fit very clearly into the agenda, and it wasn't quite enough stuff for um a whole section.

746
01:03:43,470 --> 01:03:46,544
And really, this is about copilot studio agents in particular.

747
01:03:46,704 --> 01:03:50,624
And the first more agent stuff piece is this concept of topics.

748
01:03:50,784 --> 01:03:59,904
Topics, again, in the new experience, are not prevalent or are not there, but in the classic experience or the standard experience, are there.

749
01:04:00,206 --> 01:04:06,384
This allows you to build kind of those natural language processing agents more effectively.

750
01:04:06,478 --> 01:04:14,144
And so you can have topics that have trigger descriptions, and the agent can decide when to call a topic, much like it can be configured to call a tool.

751
01:04:14,224 --> 01:04:17,664
And it will follow a mapped conversation flow that you can create.

752
01:04:17,838 --> 01:04:23,744
So you can create message nodes and question nodes and have conditions if the person responded yes or the person responded no.

753
01:04:23,918 --> 01:04:25,744
You can have it redirect to other topics.

754
01:04:25,904 --> 01:04:32,304
Really, it's this idea of how we give our LLM like a structured conversation path in the event that we need it.

755
01:04:32,384 --> 01:04:37,824
This helps us combine all of those things to create those guided conversational experiences.

756
01:04:37,984 --> 01:04:38,784
We have triggers.

757
01:04:39,054 --> 01:04:43,744
So when we talk about autonomous agents, I mentioned that you can build autonomous agents in Copilot Studio.

758
01:04:43,824 --> 01:04:46,064
That is because we have the ability to create triggers.

759
01:04:46,222 --> 01:04:50,784
So if you're familiar with process automation or power automate flows, it's the exact same concept.

760
01:04:50,944 --> 01:05:06,224
So instead of requiring somebody to talk to our agent, you can have the system or an event happen that calls and wakes up your agent, like the receipt of an email with a certain subject line or the creation of a case or the submission of an IT ticket.

761
01:05:06,304 --> 01:05:16,384
These are events that are like data driven that can trigger an agent or a again, a model with instructions, access to tools and knowledge to wake up and start working.

762
01:05:16,704 --> 01:05:19,024
And so we have the ability to use triggers to create those.

763
01:05:19,184 --> 01:05:20,704
We have these things called skills.

764
01:05:20,864 --> 01:05:26,384
Skills are essentially additional instructions, but they almost work as knowledge.

765
01:05:26,574 --> 01:05:34,544
And so what I mean by that is when you build an agent, everything you put in your instructions get passed to the LLM every single time.

766
01:05:34,704 --> 01:05:44,624
And so when I chat to an agent, the thing that's actually going to the model is my prompt and the instructions of that agent, as well as the context of our conversation.

767
01:05:44,814 --> 01:05:50,544
Then it goes and finds any knowledge and takes all of this information and then sends it to the model to produce its output.

768
01:05:50,704 --> 01:05:59,664
There could be a scenario where instructions only apply when people only talk about one specific type of task or one specific type of message.

769
01:05:59,824 --> 01:06:01,264
Then we can create a skill.

770
01:06:01,434 --> 01:06:04,234
The agent will know, ooh, they're asking about such and such.

771
01:06:04,334 --> 01:06:06,334
I have a skill that helps me do such and such.

772
01:06:06,464 --> 01:06:12,784
Let me go check out these instructions and add them essentially to my knowledge for me to understand a little bit better.

773
01:06:12,944 --> 01:06:21,664
As opposed to just including those in your instructions every single time, when 90% of the time they're talking about other tasks that don't need those relevant instructions.

774
01:06:21,984 --> 01:06:22,944
That is what skills are.

775
01:06:23,024 --> 01:06:23,904
These are markdown files.

776
01:06:23,984 --> 01:06:24,784
You can add to your agent.

777
01:06:24,944 --> 01:06:28,384
Skills only exist currently in the new copilot studio experience.

778
01:06:28,544 --> 01:06:30,944
They do not exist in the classic copilot studio experience.

779
01:06:31,104 --> 01:06:33,424
The last thing is this concept of channels.

780
01:06:33,584 --> 01:06:36,944
Channels is how people can talk to your agent.

781
01:06:37,104 --> 01:06:47,024
And so once you build an agent, say you build it in Copilot Studio and you want to publish it to Microsoft Copilot or to Microsoft Teams where people can now chat with your agent.

782
01:06:47,184 --> 01:06:51,024
You can add that channel, it's called and publish your agent to that channel.

783
01:06:51,184 --> 01:06:56,864
You can also publish to public websites if you wanted to have it be on your website and answer questions that website visitors have.

784
01:06:57,024 --> 01:07:03,744
You can publish it to like WhatsApp and Twilio and Facebook if you know your organization wants to interact with people through those services.

785
01:07:03,904 --> 01:07:09,904
You build your agent and then you can deploy it to either no channels if you don't want people to talk to it, say it's an autonomous agent.

786
01:07:10,064 --> 01:07:12,624
You can deploy it to one or many channels as well.

787
01:07:12,864 --> 01:07:17,412
when you deploy it to many channels, all those people interacting in those different channels are talking to the same agent.

788
01:07:17,504 --> 01:07:20,304
It's not like you're kind of copying your agent or things like that.

789
01:07:20,494 --> 01:07:23,744
It's all just kind of how the world interacts with your agent.

790
01:07:23,904 --> 01:07:31,984
When building an agent, if I were to summarize what we're talking about, when building an agent, it's always important to consider the limitations that are outside of the agent, all right?

791
01:07:32,064 --> 01:07:34,624
Like policies and the guardrails in place.

792
01:07:34,784 --> 01:07:42,864
You will absolutely get questions around like, hey, you want to build an agent that connects to X, Y, and Z, but this is blocked by their governance committee.

793
01:07:42,944 --> 01:07:43,664
What should you do?

794
01:07:43,824 --> 01:07:45,732
A build the agent anyways.

795
01:07:46,478 --> 01:07:49,504
No, you should not just build the agent anyways, all right?

796
01:07:49,664 --> 01:07:53,664
We should always be, you know, for the sake of the exam and in the real world, right?

797
01:07:53,744 --> 01:07:57,984
We should always be considering the policies and the guardrails in place.

798
01:07:58,144 --> 01:08:04,544
So filter every question you have through this lens, through policies, guardrails, compliance.

799
01:08:04,814 --> 01:08:09,664
We're gonna talk about responsible AI here in a moment, but through those lenses.

800
01:08:09,824 --> 01:08:14,784
The next thing you need to be aware of are these things called Azure AI services.

801
01:08:14,926 --> 01:08:23,904
And these kind of build on top of the tools concept when we talk about agents, specifically now inside of the context of Foundry.

802
01:08:24,064 --> 01:08:32,864
And really, these Azure AI services are specific tools that we can now add to our agents that serve a very specific purpose.

803
01:08:32,944 --> 01:08:36,304
And it'll make sense here in a second when I kind of show these to you.

804
01:08:36,464 --> 01:08:43,344
And really, these are, as it says here, a collection of pre-built, customizable AI services inside of Azure.

805
01:08:43,534 --> 01:08:57,252
This helps us do things like have tools for vision, for language, for search, for document processing, and those, those sort of pieces, things like translation, things like personal identifiable information, those sort of pieces.

806
01:08:57,454 --> 01:09:00,384
And so, what are these things that I'm necessarily talking about?

807
01:09:00,544 --> 01:09:10,304
It is Azure AI speech, it is content understanding, it is Azure AI Search, it is Azure Language, it is Azure Translator, which a lot of these are exactly as they sound.

808
01:09:10,464 --> 01:09:17,024
We already talked about Azure AI Search, but if you need your agent to understand natural language, then you can leverage Azure Language.

809
01:09:17,104 --> 01:09:20,224
If you needed to be able to translate, you can leverage Azure Translate.

810
01:09:20,398 --> 01:09:24,784
If you needed to be able to understand speech or to produce speech, Azure Speech.

811
01:09:24,974 --> 01:09:30,784
If you needed to see things, whether being photos or whether being like real-time streaming, Azure Vision.

812
01:09:30,944 --> 01:09:46,704
Content understanding and document intelligence is how we can get more specific capabilities outside of like invoices or receipts, or for content understanding, if you know we want it to be able to process images, audio, video, and documents, it can do all of those things better.

813
01:09:46,864 --> 01:09:51,504
And so you don't really need to know, you know, these different capabilities in detail.

814
01:09:51,664 --> 01:09:53,904
You mostly just need to know that they exist.

815
01:09:54,094 --> 01:10:00,784
If you've already got the AI 103, then you know these capabilities more than needed for this exam.

816
01:10:00,974 --> 01:10:05,424
But really, just understanding what are these tools, when should we potentially use them?

817
01:10:05,614 --> 01:10:06,464
These were brought up.

818
01:10:06,816 --> 01:10:14,256
Pretty regularly inside of the Microsoft Learn modules, but I didn't even necessarily remember getting a specific question on any of these in particular.

819
01:10:14,542 --> 01:10:16,896
So again, you know, just take it with a grain of salt.

820
01:10:17,056 --> 01:10:21,856
I think the naming of these generally tells us what they do and the business value that they provide.

821
01:10:22,158 --> 01:10:27,856
The final thing to talk about, just as a part of agent fundamentals, is this concept of agent orchestration.

822
01:10:28,078 --> 01:10:31,696
And so we talked about how we can connect agents to other agents.

823
01:10:31,886 --> 01:10:34,976
Well, I want to elaborate on that principle a little bit more.

824
01:10:35,214 --> 01:10:43,536
And so agent orchestration is the process of coordinating multiple specialized tools, capabilities, and AI agents to work together.

825
01:10:43,758 --> 01:10:47,056
So say you have a business process that has multiple stages.

826
01:10:47,216 --> 01:10:57,136
Instead of having one agent to try to handle all of these stages, it may look like something where one agent handles the first stage and one agent handles the second stage and one agent handles the third stage.

827
01:10:57,296 --> 01:11:02,256
And this idea of agent orchestration is this connected web of agents.

828
01:11:02,416 --> 01:11:06,816
If you are maybe not understanding the term orchestration, think of it like an orchestra, right?

829
01:11:06,896 --> 01:11:11,936
Like this is the idea that we have a group of agents producing one goal.

830
01:11:12,096 --> 01:11:15,776
Or think of it like a group of agents or a just like a flock of birds.

831
01:11:15,936 --> 01:11:18,256
We have an orchestration of agents.

832
01:11:18,416 --> 01:11:21,056
Like that's that's really the idea behind this.

833
01:11:21,216 --> 01:11:23,696
And so we have single agent systems, right?

834
01:11:23,776 --> 01:11:27,268
That is there's one agent, but then we also have multi-agent systems.

835
01:11:27,376 --> 01:11:33,296
And really, multi-agent systems are valuable because it allows us to have a separation of concerns.

836
01:11:33,456 --> 01:11:38,656
What that means is each agent focuses on a distinct domain or responsibility.

837
01:11:38,816 --> 01:11:43,136
This also gives us really clear boundaries for like security and compliance.

838
01:11:43,310 --> 01:11:52,176
So, say, for example, you're met with a scenario where there is some kind of business process or group of people that could work in different compliance boundaries.

839
01:11:52,336 --> 01:11:56,176
How can we leverage a multi-agent system to meet that compliant?

840
01:11:56,336 --> 01:11:57,776
And so there are trade-offs, right?

841
01:11:57,856 --> 01:12:02,016
I'm not necessarily saying that multi-agent is always better than single agent.

842
01:12:02,256 --> 01:12:12,896
Multi-agent means IT admins now have to monitor and document and keep track of and update and fix bugs on two agents instead of just one, right?

843
01:12:13,136 --> 01:12:18,576
But what that means is it may be more scalable, it may have better performance and those different things.

844
01:12:18,736 --> 01:12:26,016
There is a trade-off with latency as well, because again, information has to be passed from one agent to the next and then reprocessed, so to speak.

845
01:12:26,176 --> 01:12:27,056
That idea, right?

846
01:12:27,296 --> 01:12:28,816
How quickly it can happen.

847
01:12:28,976 --> 01:12:33,856
But, you know, things to consider as we're solution architects designing our agentic solutions.

848
01:12:33,998 --> 01:12:39,536
And so I've mentioned a couple of times this concept of like tasks being very siloed or thin, right?

849
01:12:39,726 --> 01:12:45,776
And finding agents that meet those specific needs for a specific point in the business process.

850
01:12:45,936 --> 01:13:03,536
And so, like, if we're thinking about if we're wanting to have an agent, this was kind of applied specifically to Copilot Studio, but if we're wanting to add dozens of tools or multiple multi-step things to our agent, then we should be looking at, hey, how can we segment this agent out into multiple different parts?

851
01:13:03,758 --> 01:13:10,656
And there's kind of different, different ways or types of orchestration that we're going to introduce here in a moment and walk through that you need to be aware of.

852
01:13:10,816 --> 01:13:13,376
And now just know, like this is more of a design principle.

853
01:13:13,486 --> 01:13:14,816
This isn't like a hard limit.

854
01:13:14,896 --> 01:13:19,216
But as we think about like this idea of, okay, this agent is starting to get pretty big.

855
01:13:19,376 --> 01:13:28,416
We've got a dozen tools, we've got a dozen knowledge sources, we've got a handful of topics, we're publishing it to different channels, it's servicing multiple people in multiple regions.

856
01:13:28,576 --> 01:13:30,496
How can we maybe segment this out?

857
01:13:30,576 --> 01:13:44,256
Because you know, again, when we talk about trade-offs, it's also important to think about the fact that like if we just have one agent that does all this, that also, yes, it's less to manage and less to monitor, like in terms of operations, but it's also means you only you have one point of failure now.

858
01:13:44,430 --> 01:13:47,524
Like if that agent stops working, the whole business process stops.

859
01:13:47,776 --> 01:13:55,136
maybe it wouldn't be as critical if you know steps one, two, and three continue, but step four ceases to work or something in case that agent broke or something.

860
01:13:55,630 --> 01:13:55,984
Like that, right?

861
01:13:56,144 --> 01:13:59,396
So, again, these trade-offs for us to consider as solution architects.

862
01:13:59,584 --> 01:14:01,824
There are five different types of orchestration.

863
01:14:01,984 --> 01:14:11,024
You don't need to know how these work, how to set these up, the minute details of these, but you need to be aware that these different types of orchestrations exist.

864
01:14:11,278 --> 01:14:17,744
And again, I didn't even necessarily have any explicit questions on all of these in my exam, but the Microsoft Learn content brought them up.

865
01:14:17,824 --> 01:14:20,624
And I think that they're fairly simple to understand in theory.

866
01:14:20,846 --> 01:14:22,544
The first one being sequential.

867
01:14:22,784 --> 01:14:30,384
Sequential is this idea that it is step one and then it is past the next step, and then there's step two, and then it is past the next step, and it is step three.

868
01:14:30,606 --> 01:14:37,956
Essentially, each agent runs only after the previous one completes, and the order that these run in is kind of predefined.

869
01:14:38,318 --> 01:14:40,864
It's already a predefined workflow.

870
01:14:41,024 --> 01:14:43,264
If one step fails and the chain stops, right?

871
01:14:43,344 --> 01:14:46,544
Like if this agent stops working, it doesn't pass anything on to this agent.

872
01:14:46,766 --> 01:14:48,944
It kind of is like classic process automation.

873
01:14:49,184 --> 01:14:57,744
Concurrent is the same kind of concept, but now we can have independent processes run concurrently or run in parallel or run at the same time.

874
01:14:57,998 --> 01:15:06,544
So if we have steps one, two, three, and four, and steps two and three really aren't dependent on each other, we shouldn't use sequential because that's going to make our process slower.

875
01:15:06,704 --> 01:15:12,304
We could have step one and then steps two and three, and then all of that combined to step four, so to speak.

876
01:15:12,494 --> 01:15:20,100
So it kind of takes the concept of sequential, but brings some further enhancement for asynchronous steps.

877
01:15:20,464 --> 01:15:27,584
Handoff is very similar to the both of those, but handoff, it's no longer a predefined process.

878
01:15:27,790 --> 01:15:35,584
So sequential, it's you know, you as the orchestrator or the person who defined the process defines this agent, then this agent, then this agent.

879
01:15:35,744 --> 01:15:41,184
Well, handoff, what actually happens is this agent does its work and then it says, Who do I go to next?

880
01:15:41,424 --> 01:15:42,544
It says, Okay, this person.

881
01:15:42,702 --> 01:15:45,664
All right, and then this person does their work and they say, Who do I go to next?

882
01:15:45,904 --> 01:15:46,756
it says, this person.

883
01:15:47,024 --> 01:15:51,584
So one run, it may look like this, here, here, and then back up to here, and then back to here and here.

884
01:15:51,664 --> 01:15:56,144
And the next one, it may look like this and then back here, and then back here, and then here, and then here, and then here.

885
01:15:56,238 --> 01:16:00,996
It's this idea that the agent itself decides who it hands off to next.

886
01:16:01,230 --> 01:16:02,144
We have group chat.

887
01:16:02,254 --> 01:16:04,384
This is pretty simple in concept, right?

888
01:16:04,526 --> 01:16:14,704
Essentially, there's one kind of agent orchestrator in the middle, and you can surround it with agents that all contribute when it feels like it has the right time and skills to do so.

889
01:16:14,926 --> 01:16:26,544
So essentially, all of them are listening to the conversation, so to speak, or listening to the group chat, and they're all adding their two cents or adding their context to the chat.

890
01:16:26,766 --> 01:16:38,304
And so, when this is really going to be beneficial, are those super highly collaborative steps in a business process where things need to consider a whole bunch of different pieces of information, how that can be done.

891
01:16:38,384 --> 01:16:43,104
And the final output is ultimately derived from the group discussion as a whole.

892
01:16:43,424 --> 01:16:44,324
Magentic.

893
01:16:44,974 --> 01:16:47,012
not to be confused with like magnetic.

894
01:16:47,344 --> 01:16:50,784
I always read it that way, but I believe it's pronounced magentic.

895
01:16:50,864 --> 01:16:54,384
And this is kind of like handoff, kind of like group chat.

896
01:16:54,606 --> 01:16:59,584
Essentially, what it means is there's like a manager agent, and this is kind of our agent orchestration layer.

897
01:16:59,694 --> 01:17:02,624
And what it does is it say calls agent A.

898
01:17:02,798 --> 01:17:04,624
Agent A reasons and then responds.

899
01:17:04,704 --> 01:17:07,344
And it says, hmm, let me call agent B now, right?

900
01:17:07,424 --> 01:17:09,024
And then it calls agent B and then it responds.

901
01:17:09,230 --> 01:17:11,344
It says, hmm, let me call agent B again, right?

902
01:17:11,424 --> 01:17:13,732
And then it calls agent B again and then responds.

903
01:17:13,824 --> 01:17:16,384
And so that's really the concept of magentic.

904
01:17:16,494 --> 01:17:25,024
It's this kind of like delegation to do its work, but ultimately the manager agent is the one reasoning over all of the information and producing the final answer.

905
01:17:25,230 --> 01:17:30,144
The final concept in terms of agent orchestration is this idea of agent-to-agent protocol.

906
01:17:30,304 --> 01:17:35,664
And so, really, what this means is you have the ability to call other pre-built agents.

907
01:17:35,984 --> 01:17:43,264
if you're building agents inside of Copilot Studio or Foundry, there can be kind of the low-code or intuitive ways to connect them.

908
01:17:43,374 --> 01:17:50,144
But you also have the ability, just like you could build any kind of custom connector, you could call any kind of agent available.

909
01:17:50,224 --> 01:17:59,024
This is kind of a standard where, if an organization has built an agent where it wants other agents to talk to it, a standard that organizations follow and abide by.

910
01:17:59,118 --> 01:18:05,904
And so, really, this allows you to call virtually any third-party pre-built agent that you necessarily need.

911
01:18:06,030 --> 01:18:22,064
You don't need to know how to configure these or know the details of this standard, but you essentially just need to know that you have this thing called agent to agent protocol that exists in case there's already an agent that maybe is outside of the Microsoft ecosystem, the UN agent inside the Microsoft ecosystem to be able to call and talk to.

912
01:18:22,158 --> 01:18:24,036
You can leverage agent to agent protocol.

913
01:18:24,304 --> 01:18:30,592
The final part of This section, again, the largest is responsible AI principles.

914
01:18:30,752 --> 01:18:40,192
I think it's more than fair to assume that if you are at this point, you have been made aware of these responsible AI principles by any of the prerequisite exams.

915
01:18:40,352 --> 01:18:50,672
Virtually every AI exam I have taken, Microsoft AI exam I have taken, have hit on these responsible AI principles in some kind of way, whether large or small.

916
01:18:50,912 --> 01:19:02,112
And really, these are the six guiding principles that Microsoft abides by in terms of everything that it is building, as well as encourages everybody to build inside of its platform.

917
01:19:02,272 --> 01:19:11,632
And so these are the North Star, so to speak, around making sure that our AI systems perform as intended, that they perform ethically, that they perform correctly.

918
01:19:11,792 --> 01:19:12,992
And there's six principles.

919
01:19:13,102 --> 01:19:21,472
I'll read them very briefly, and that is fairness, reliability and safety, privacy and security, inclusiveness, transparency, and accountability.

920
01:19:21,712 --> 01:19:24,992
Fairness is this idea that our agent is not biased.

921
01:19:25,166 --> 01:19:34,432
If our agent is making decisions, that it is not, you know, being unequitable towards anybody that may be using it or an impact of its use.

922
01:19:34,592 --> 01:19:40,352
A lot of times, any biasness caused or lack of fairness in agents, it's actually due to the data.

923
01:19:40,526 --> 01:19:44,912
And so when we talk about good data, it's making sure that it's representative and fair.

924
01:19:45,072 --> 01:19:55,232
Reliability and safety means that our agent passes testing and acts in accordance reliably to the standards or the main purpose and scope of the agent.

925
01:19:55,392 --> 01:19:58,352
Now, obviously, agents have the ability to hallucinate.

926
01:19:58,446 --> 01:20:07,472
This isn't necessarily a call that it is perfect, so to speak, but it is this call that hey, we need to make sure that our agents are secure and that our agents are reliable.

927
01:20:07,694 --> 01:20:18,272
And really, when it's talking about this in terms of safety, it's meaning meaning more so in terms of that it doesn't produce harmful content or explicit content or like sexual content or anything like that.

928
01:20:18,432 --> 01:20:20,032
But it works reliably.

929
01:20:20,206 --> 01:20:22,352
It calls the tools that it's supposed to call.

930
01:20:22,542 --> 01:20:27,072
It doesn't have access to the things that shouldn't be able to access, and it doesn't produce harmful content.

931
01:20:27,232 --> 01:20:29,552
Privacy and security is exactly as it sounds.

932
01:20:29,632 --> 01:20:49,152
It's this idea that agents don't allow people to circumvent any sort of existing security infrastructure, that it practices proper encryption and adherence to global standards, especially in terms of data residency or certain compliance needs based off of a certain region an organization may be in, making sure that our AI solutions are abiding by those.

933
01:20:49,312 --> 01:20:51,792
Inclusiveness is a little bit different than fairness.

934
01:20:51,952 --> 01:20:59,472
Inclusiveness essentially means that everybody has the ability to use an AI solution, say for their job.

935
01:20:59,726 --> 01:21:13,152
So, for example, if we're building an agent that supports our sales organization, say we have a member on our team who is visually impaired, how we can curate our agent to also have voice capabilities to include that person's ability to use it.

936
01:21:13,312 --> 01:21:20,192
It's this the AI tools that we are building are accessible and bring equitable outcomes for all users, regardless of background.

937
01:21:20,352 --> 01:21:35,952
Transparency is this idea that agents are not a black box, that we can see how agents are producing their answer, how they're getting their response, and also all the traceability around this agent was talked to from this person and it then connected to these systems.

938
01:21:36,046 --> 01:21:38,432
That it is transparent into how it is working.

939
01:21:38,512 --> 01:21:49,312
And accountability is ultimately that people and organizations are responsible for the agents that they develop and the agents that they use and the downstream effects of those agent uses.

940
01:21:49,472 --> 01:21:50,832
That wraps up that section.

941
01:21:51,072 --> 01:21:57,312
This brings us to our third section around some of the pre built agent capabilities.

