02:10:47,728 --> 02:10:51,140
And it had 30% fewer manual tasks than a workflow.

1512
02:10:51,248 --> 02:10:53,540
And it completed those tasks 20% faster.

1513
02:10:53,728 --> 02:10:57,088
And AI had recommended accuracy of 85%.

1514
02:10:57,550 --> 02:10:58,448
That's plenty good to us.

1515
02:10:58,670 --> 02:11:03,396
100% of outputs audited or showed up in the monitoring tools for governance.

1516
02:11:03,488 --> 02:11:06,608
And it has the ability to handle up to 10,000 requests a day.

1517
02:11:06,688 --> 02:11:08,448
Like it's scalable for our organization.

1518
02:11:08,608 --> 02:11:12,448
And so these are the success criteria that we like to see for agents.

1519
02:11:12,608 --> 02:11:16,452
Again, even before we deploy them, before we even finish development.

1520
02:11:16,688 --> 02:11:22,368
There's kind of some different tests to test or some different things to test that are important to know.

1521
02:11:22,528 --> 02:11:29,088
And so, specifically, this is really important because there's kind of different metrics in terms of testing that you need to be aware of.

1522
02:11:29,168 --> 02:11:31,328
And they're a little bit different than monitoring metrics.

1523
02:11:31,502 --> 02:11:32,848
I want to outline what these are.

1524
02:11:33,008 --> 02:11:40,048
A lot of times this can apply more specifically to like foundry evaluations if you're familiar with agents inside of foundry.

1525
02:11:40,128 --> 02:11:43,808
But really, this is the idea of what are we testing very specifically?

1526
02:11:43,968 --> 02:11:50,128
Because you can't just generally you can you can't just generally test your agent, but you want to test specific things.

1527
02:11:50,448 --> 02:11:54,848
There's different considerations that we may need to test, and this applies to the testing scope.

1528
02:11:55,008 --> 02:11:56,928
So the first one is scenario based testing.

1529
02:11:57,038 --> 02:12:07,968
So this is validating that the real world business processes and end to end user journeys are correct, that it's finding the right edge cases, that it's calling the right knowledge sources, that what the knowledge sources are retrieving are correct.

1530
02:12:08,334 --> 02:12:11,184
Right, that it's completing the common user scenarios.

1531
02:12:11,344 --> 02:12:13,904
Performance and reliability testing is a little bit different.

1532
02:12:14,064 --> 02:12:16,224
This we're testing, how long does it take to respond?

1533
02:12:16,366 --> 02:12:17,424
Does it take 10 seconds?

1534
02:12:17,584 --> 02:12:18,756
Does it take three minutes?

1535
02:12:19,024 --> 02:12:21,504
How long does this take to respond?

1536
02:12:21,584 --> 02:12:25,664
Especially in consideration if we have a whole lot of people talking to it concurrently.

1537
02:12:25,824 --> 02:12:30,304
That lays into the throughput of the model, the success rate and failure rates of the model.

1538
02:12:30,510 --> 02:12:32,224
What kind of token efficiency do we have?

1539
02:12:32,398 --> 02:12:36,384
Maybe we have an agent that's going to be used by a lot of people and have to consume a lot of information.

1540
02:12:36,624 --> 02:12:44,932
Could we get 99% of the same efficiency with a model that's twice as token efficient, basically being half the price?

1541
02:12:45,104 --> 02:12:45,824
How can we do that?

1542
02:12:46,030 --> 02:12:48,224
Also, looking at any model drift indicators.

1543
02:12:48,384 --> 02:12:55,024
Model drift is this concept of last month it worked really well, but now this month it works like 10% worse.

1544
02:12:55,184 --> 02:12:56,464
Why did that necessarily happen?

1545
02:12:56,544 --> 02:12:57,984
That could be because of model drift.

1546
02:12:58,144 --> 02:13:13,184
Integration testing is right, if we're connecting our agents to knowledge or tools or other third-party agents or custom connectors and APIs, how are we making sure that those connections are verified, that they are secure, that they're using Azure Key Vault and if not, managed identities, right?

1547
02:13:13,344 --> 02:13:15,264
How are we testing all of those things?

1548
02:13:15,424 --> 02:13:23,104
If you know something were to break on a third-party service, how do we make sure that it's our agent doesn't break or a business process doesn't fail, right?

1549
02:13:23,184 --> 02:13:29,984
How do we make sure, even if that's a mission critical thing, that we're handling those blackouts or errors that come back that might not be in our control?

1550
02:13:30,144 --> 02:13:32,064
Behavioral and quality testing.

1551
02:13:32,270 --> 02:13:34,784
This is like, hey, I asked you to do X.

1552
02:13:34,944 --> 02:13:36,224
Did you start doing Y?

1553
02:13:36,544 --> 02:13:36,784
Right?

1554
02:13:36,864 --> 02:13:38,224
Or did you actually do X?

1555
02:13:38,384 --> 02:13:42,544
What you did was accurate and relevant to my question or my prompt.

1556
02:13:42,704 --> 02:13:46,624
Conversation quality is a lot of times around like, is it coherent?

1557
02:13:46,784 --> 02:13:47,504
Is it fluent?

1558
02:13:47,664 --> 02:13:49,824
Like, is it speaking my language correctly?

1559
02:13:50,062 --> 02:13:53,264
Especially if we're using like small language models, maybe it might not.

1560
02:13:53,424 --> 02:13:59,184
A lot of the large language models generally have no problem today with coherence and fluency and those sort of things.

1561
02:13:59,278 --> 02:14:00,944
But what kind of knowledge coverage do we have?

1562
02:14:01,104 --> 02:14:01,824
Or are we missing?

1563
02:14:01,934 --> 02:14:08,064
Like, is it am I asking about a certain topic that this agent should handle that it doesn't actually have a knowledge source to support that work?

1564
02:14:08,238 --> 02:14:10,784
Maybe the tone and as well as the output format.

1565
02:14:10,864 --> 02:14:15,424
Should it be outputting in paragraphs, bullet points, tables, JSON, yes and no Booleans?

1566
02:14:15,504 --> 02:14:16,544
Like, how should it be outputting?

1567
02:14:16,718 --> 02:14:17,744
Safety and compliance.

1568
02:14:17,838 --> 02:14:19,664
I think this is self-explanatory, right?

1569
02:14:19,758 --> 02:14:25,744
How effective are the guardrails at checking harmful content, sensitive data protection, security and compliance requirements?

1570
02:14:25,904 --> 02:14:26,944
then usability testing.

1571
02:14:27,024 --> 02:14:31,024
This is very similar to scenario-based testing, but this is how usable is it?

1572
02:14:31,184 --> 02:14:33,184
What's the user satisfaction, right?

1573
02:14:33,264 --> 02:14:36,864
Do they rank it a one out of five stars or a five out of five stars?

1574
02:14:37,024 --> 02:14:39,104
What kind of feedback loops do we have, right?

1575
02:14:39,424 --> 02:14:41,504
We can thumbs up or thumbs down questions.

1576
02:14:41,678 --> 02:14:44,784
There's regular surveys where we ask people to submit their feedback.

1577
02:14:44,944 --> 02:14:47,424
How common is an actual task being completed?

1578
02:14:47,584 --> 02:14:51,984
If somebody says, hey, I want to draft an email, how often is an email actually getting drafted?

1579
02:14:52,144 --> 02:14:55,344
All of those sort of things go into those sort of metrics.

1580
02:14:55,598 --> 02:14:57,700
So this is all kind of a part of testing scope, right?

1581
02:14:57,904 --> 02:14:58,864
What are we testing?

1582
02:14:58,944 --> 02:15:02,464
But as we know, the other part of test cases are testing data.

1583
02:15:02,606 --> 02:15:04,304
There's actually two different types of data sets.

1584
02:15:04,464 --> 02:15:08,464
And before this exam, I wasn't even familiar with these two different terminologies.

1585
02:15:08,544 --> 02:15:09,984
So it's important for you to know.

1586
02:15:10,144 --> 02:15:14,224
And essentially, we obviously know if we're going to test well, we need different types of data.

1587
02:15:14,384 --> 02:15:15,504
We need accurate data.

1588
02:15:15,598 --> 02:15:18,784
And so there's different different types of data sets you need to be aware of.

1589
02:15:18,944 --> 02:15:22,304
A red data set is something that is mutable and experimental.

1590
02:15:22,464 --> 02:15:27,904
Essentially, what that means is this is like the the development or testing and experimentation.

1591
02:15:28,110 --> 02:15:36,624
So this might not necessarily have like this might not be a data set that might be like super representative of, say, all of your accounts or your contacts or your leads or something like that.

1592
02:15:36,784 --> 02:15:40,704
But what this is is, you know, this is something that is flexible and changeable.

1593
02:15:40,784 --> 02:15:43,184
It's not approved for production decision making.

1594
02:15:43,344 --> 02:15:46,144
It may contain evolving schemas, you know, things like that.

1595
02:15:46,224 --> 02:15:48,944
It's a it's our prototyping, it's our red data set.

1596
02:15:49,038 --> 02:15:55,488
Whereas opposed to our gold data sets, what these are are like Frozen data sets as a source of truth.

1597
02:15:55,662 --> 02:15:59,888
So, for example, when we talked about the agent lifecycle, there was a staging step.

1598
02:16:00,048 --> 02:16:05,488
This staging step before production is where we can first test our agent against a gold data set.

1599
02:16:05,568 --> 02:16:10,448
What this may look like is a copy of the production data into the UAT environment.

1600
02:16:10,528 --> 02:16:13,968
And then we leverage our agent talking to the copy of the production data.

1601
02:16:14,048 --> 02:16:15,648
So it's not necessarily this, right?

1602
02:16:15,728 --> 02:16:22,608
Where this is experimental, might not be representative, it might not be a full set, but this is actually like production data, so to speak.

1603
02:16:22,688 --> 02:16:24,848
But we're not necessarily able to, you know, it's frozen.

1604
02:16:24,928 --> 02:16:30,928
We're not necessarily trying to change production data, but we're trying to use it for testing, benchmarking, and deployment.

1605
02:16:31,150 --> 02:16:36,448
So this idea, you know, if we if we wanted to think about red being play around with it, experimental, no problem.

1606
02:16:36,608 --> 02:16:39,008
Gold, it's the gold standard, so to speak, right?

1607
02:16:39,150 --> 02:16:40,448
It's the production data.

1608
02:16:40,608 --> 02:16:43,968
This is the thing, the the two different types of data sets we could look at.

