I think it's easy to discuss or to kind of brainstorm what this means, but let's talk about it very practically what this means.

1614
02:17:02,222 --> 02:17:03,888
There are a couple of key metrics.

1615
02:17:04,078 --> 02:17:13,008
A lot of times these can be tied to the same as those testing metrics, side note, that we should be monitoring throughout the operations of our agents, right?

1616
02:17:13,088 --> 02:17:14,688
And the first one being operational health.

1617
02:17:14,768 --> 02:17:16,528
This is kind of the most general and broad.

1618
02:17:16,688 --> 02:17:27,168
We want to make sure there's no error rates or failures, that there's resiliency, what kind of escalation trends are there in terms of an agent can't figure it out, so it escalates to a human, those sort of things.

1619
02:17:27,374 --> 02:17:28,768
We have performance metrics.

1620
02:17:28,848 --> 02:17:30,928
So these are exactly the same as the performance test.

1621
02:17:31,054 --> 02:17:34,768
We're testing how quickly it responds, what its throughput is, what its success rate is.

1622
02:17:35,008 --> 02:17:36,128
Resource utilization.

1623
02:17:36,288 --> 02:17:41,888
This essentially, hey, if we've connected it to a bunch of tools, but it only uses like one of the tools, why?

1624
02:17:42,062 --> 02:17:44,608
Why is it only utilizing one of those resources?

1625
02:17:44,768 --> 02:17:49,220
Would it be more efficient if it used multiple of those resources as well as that model drift?

1626
02:17:49,408 --> 02:17:56,768
Quality and output accuracy is the response accuracy, is again this concept that if I asked about X, did it respond about X, right?

1627
02:17:56,848 --> 02:17:57,728
Or did it respond about Y?

1628
02:17:57,968 --> 02:17:59,328
Did it start talking about something else?

1629
02:17:59,438 --> 02:18:03,328
Or did it come back and say an incorrect answer about X, right?

1630
02:18:03,488 --> 02:18:04,208
That idea.

1631
02:18:04,368 --> 02:18:07,648
Knowledge coverage is very similar to this resource utilization.

1632
02:18:07,808 --> 02:18:18,048
This idea that we have all of the knowledge that we need our agent to have access to, that it has access to those things, its action effectiveness and its output completeness and relevance.

1633
02:18:18,208 --> 02:18:21,956
Usage highlights is around how many active users, how many conversations are happening?

1634
02:18:22,128 --> 02:18:27,168
What are the most common tools that are being called or knowledge sources that are being retrieved by our agents?

1635
02:18:27,342 --> 02:18:30,448
What are the most common topics for our copilot Studio agents that are being triggered?

1636
02:18:30,670 --> 02:18:33,088
What sort of features in our agents are being utilized the most?

1637
02:18:33,262 --> 02:18:34,724
And which ones are not being utilized?

1638
02:18:34,928 --> 02:18:37,328
What sort of departments are adopting our agents?

1639
02:18:37,488 --> 02:18:39,168
What sort of people are adopting our agents?

1640
02:18:39,310 --> 02:18:42,048
What sort of departments or people are not adopting our agents?

1641
02:18:42,208 --> 02:18:44,928
All of that sort of usage stuff, user experience.

1642
02:18:45,038 --> 02:18:48,928
Again, this is based off of that uh that usage testing capabilities.

1643
02:18:49,102 --> 02:18:52,208
How many times are tasks being completed versus actually being abandoned?

1644
02:18:52,288 --> 02:18:54,448
How many times are they being escalated to a human?

1645
02:18:54,638 --> 02:18:56,368
What are our CSAT scores, right?

1646
02:18:56,448 --> 02:18:58,768
Are our user satisfied use scores?

1647
02:18:58,894 --> 02:19:01,888
How many times are people liking or disliking a message?

1648
02:19:02,208 --> 02:19:04,688
are we getting five stars, three stars, or one stars?

1649
02:19:04,848 --> 02:19:07,168
All sorts of survey or feedback sort of trends.

1650
02:19:07,248 --> 02:19:10,128
And then also we have any kind of risk compliance and security.

1651
02:19:10,288 --> 02:19:15,648
There's all sorts of different tools to be able to track these different things that I'm going to showcase here in just a moment.

1652
02:19:15,808 --> 02:19:20,448
I also wanted to highlight though, before we do that, an AI troubleshooting step.

1653
02:19:20,528 --> 02:19:20,768
All right.

1654
02:19:20,878 --> 02:19:23,488
So again, if we think about as an architect, you're you're a map.

1655
02:19:24,110 --> 02:19:24,528
person, right?

1656
02:19:24,608 --> 02:19:25,636
You're a design person.

1657
02:19:25,728 --> 02:19:27,076
You're a blueprinting person.

1658
02:19:27,168 --> 02:19:31,808
You need to be able to think about like, hey, if we're having some kind of issue, what is the likely cause?

1659
02:19:32,014 --> 02:19:35,460
Like how do we map to a how do we troubleshoot that thing?

1660
02:19:35,568 --> 02:19:37,968
How do we map an issue to a resolution?

1661
02:19:38,158 --> 02:19:43,248
And so I kind of wanted to just highlight some of these, read some of these that I took from the Microsoft Learn documentation.

1662
02:19:43,408 --> 02:19:46,848
Where say we're having a model or a prompt issue, what could that mean?

1663
02:19:47,086 --> 02:19:50,528
Maybe it means we have poor instructions or people are sending unclear prompts.

1664
02:19:50,734 --> 02:19:52,128
Say we have knowledge gaps, right?

1665
02:19:52,368 --> 02:19:55,168
It's giving an incorrect response like this is not right.

1666
02:19:55,374 --> 02:20:01,008
All the likely causes a knowledge gap, which means it could be having missing, outdated or incomplete grounding data.

1667
02:20:01,168 --> 02:20:04,848
There's a knowledge source we need to update or remove or add to our agent.

1668
02:20:05,102 --> 02:20:06,848
Say we have integration failures, right?

1669
02:20:06,928 --> 02:20:11,248
We're connecting to this third party agent, we're connecting to this third party service and it's not working.

1670
02:20:11,408 --> 02:20:24,448
We need to understand why that is as in say we're having frequent restarts or failures or you know our audit logs seem to be you know our our CSAT has gone down tremendously because it starts responding I don't know how to answer that because a integration is failing.

1671
02:20:24,608 --> 02:20:27,088
Say we have more like configuration type issues.

1672
02:20:27,248 --> 02:20:31,328
This is going to be around permissions, roles, environment variables, stuff like that.

1673
02:20:31,502 --> 02:20:37,728
Or say somebody keeps trying to talk to an agent and says sorry I can't do that or you don't have the access to do that.

1674
02:20:37,902 --> 02:20:51,568
This is caused because some kind of governance control blocked that should we review our security policies our DLP controls our compliance restrictions or should we update our agent to not allow this sort of blocked action because the majority of people have those sort of things.

1675
02:20:51,728 --> 02:21:03,488
And so this idea we need to not just understand what are the metrics and what our agents we need to have a deeper level understanding of what all of those things, especially in a scenario based type question.

1676
02:21:03,648 --> 02:21:13,648
We have all sorts of monitoring tools this isn't even an exhaustive list in here but some that are called out that are really important to know and some of these were brought up on my exam.

