02:25:13,840 --> 02:25:18,640
Again, another monitoring tool, I wouldn't classify it with any of these per se.

1710
02:25:18,800 --> 02:25:23,360
These are going to be more like enterprise-wide across different agent type systems.

1711
02:25:23,520 --> 02:25:29,120
But nonetheless, you know, for foundry agents, we have the foundry control plane as well, which has some different capabilities.

1712
02:25:29,294 --> 02:25:36,080
You don't necessarily need to know, like I said, all the nuances of the foundry control plane, but just know that it exists so you're not caught off guard on the exam.

1713
02:25:36,270 --> 02:25:48,800
The final piece to wrap up this section is again an operating model around how do we put the right operations, the people, the roles, the tasks in place to make sure monitoring actually happens.

1714
02:25:48,974 --> 02:25:58,240
And so this combines clear ownership, consistent monitoring practices, structured governance, and continuous improvement to ensure agents remain reliable, secure, and aligned with business objectives.

1715
02:25:58,400 --> 02:26:07,920
Specifically, what this means is that this stuff is assigned out, that we have clear roles and responsibility for who's in charge of monitoring the different agent monitoring tools.

1716
02:26:08,080 --> 02:26:24,320
You know, hey, your job is to review the audit logs in purview, and your job is to review the login information in M365, and your job is to audit what agents are published in agent 365 in the M365 admin center.

1717
02:26:24,400 --> 02:26:36,320
And your job is to, when a problem is detected on a copilot studio agent, to go into that copilot studio agent analytics and assess and determine what that is and reach out to the agent owner to remediate that issue, right?

1718
02:26:36,462 --> 02:26:37,440
Like this is the idea.

1719
02:26:37,582 --> 02:26:50,720
And as a solutions architect, we don't just need to be aware of what agents exist or what monitoring tools exist, but also know how we can empower either our organizations or the organizations we work with on how to use those things effectively.

1720
02:26:50,894 --> 02:26:56,320
And so, really, it means we combine the people, the process, the metrics, and the improvement all together.

1721
02:26:56,400 --> 02:27:06,000
Again, you don't necessarily need to know the, you know, what are the four pieces of an operating model, but it's this idea that we need an operations model in terms of monitoring our agents.

1722
02:27:06,222 --> 02:27:10,640
All right, the last and final section, this is three slides, I believe you can hang in there.

1723
02:27:10,800 --> 02:27:14,240
This is talking about measuring return on investment.

1724
02:27:14,400 --> 02:27:22,080
And so there are a couple of ROI drivers, the things that make up a return on investment for our agents.

1725
02:27:22,254 --> 02:27:25,840
And so one thing that you'll notice is this is not just like revenue, all right?

1726
02:27:25,920 --> 02:27:28,000
Or you'll see the first one is productivity gains.

1727
02:27:28,160 --> 02:27:32,720
A lot of people love to say, oh, this process used to take me 10 minutes and now it takes me 30 seconds with an agent.

1728
02:27:32,880 --> 02:27:35,440
That is an ROI driver that is on this list.

1729
02:27:35,662 --> 02:27:39,760
But if I'm honest, and even in the real world, that's not the one people care about the most.

1730
02:27:39,918 --> 02:27:44,080
And so there's different types of ROI drivers we need to be aware of.

1731
02:27:44,240 --> 02:27:46,560
The first one being those productivity gains, right?

1732
02:27:46,640 --> 02:27:48,560
These are the ones people are most familiar with.

1733
02:27:48,640 --> 02:27:52,160
It's a reduced manual effort, the accelerated task completion, et cetera.

1734
02:27:52,240 --> 02:27:53,360
Then we have revenue growth.

1735
02:27:53,440 --> 02:27:55,440
How do we create new revenue opportunities?

1736
02:27:55,630 --> 02:28:00,560
Instead of creating new opportunities, increase the dollar amount of our opportunities by 10%.

1737
02:28:01,040 --> 02:28:02,560
It's about how do we grow our business?

1738
02:28:02,720 --> 02:28:06,480
Maybe we create new product lines, maybe we create better marketing campaigns, et cetera.

1739
02:28:06,640 --> 02:28:11,520
Cost savings, which is different than productivity gains, all right, even though it can be similar.

1740
02:28:11,680 --> 02:28:14,960
But this ultimately is how do we reduce our operational expenses?

1741
02:28:15,120 --> 02:28:18,480
Maybe there's some kind of tool that costs a lot of money, right?

1742
02:28:18,560 --> 02:28:22,240
It's a SaaS tool and it's an expensive license your organization is paying for.

1743
02:28:22,414 --> 02:28:25,520
Hey, how can we build an agent that does this for half the price?

1744
02:28:25,742 --> 02:28:28,000
And so we have massive cost savings right there.

1745
02:28:28,160 --> 02:28:28,960
Risk reduction.

1746
02:28:29,040 --> 02:28:32,080
This is a huge one that a lot of people sleep on, all right?

1747
02:28:32,160 --> 02:28:33,520
One that people do not consider.

1748
02:28:33,710 --> 02:28:38,240
What this means is to say we have an agent that supports our legal and compliance team.

1749
02:28:38,320 --> 02:28:41,120
And currently we have a human error rate of 10%.

1750
02:28:41,600 --> 02:28:50,320
And a human error, no human error is actually likely to cause that big of a risk, but 1% of human errors costs our organization $100,000.

1751
02:28:50,640 --> 02:28:56,720
I don't know the math off the top of my head, but that essentially means every human error costs us, every human error costs us $1,000.

1752
02:28:57,296 --> 02:28:57,696
Essentially.

1753
02:28:57,856 --> 02:29:00,016
That's the risk if you break it down that way.

1754
02:29:00,206 --> 02:29:07,136
What that means is if say 10% of our business processes have a human error, excuse me, that's what I'm trying to say.

1755
02:29:07,296 --> 02:29:16,656
10% of this process has a human error, and each human error costs our organization $1,000, Well, how do we reduce our risks so that there's only two% errors, right?

1756
02:29:16,736 --> 02:29:19,456
Instead of 10%, that just reduced our risk footprint.

1757
02:29:19,536 --> 02:29:24,736
And maybe instead of now every error being $100,000, maybe now every error is $20,000.

1758
02:29:24,976 --> 02:29:38,052
So we can have a risk reduction in multiple ways, which to an organization is very valuable, even though that doesn't directly impact revenue or expenses, but it does definitely impact an organization and their return on investment.

1759
02:29:38,256 --> 02:29:40,256
Then we have strategic innovation and value.

1760
02:29:40,336 --> 02:29:50,148
And so really this is the idea about how do we be creative to grow our business in new ways, establish new competitive advantages, new innovative opportunities to support long-term organizational success.

1761
02:29:50,446 --> 02:29:59,776
So a little bit different than revenue growth, all right, but it's like how do we leverage an agent to help us think a little bit deeper or better about how we can grow our business as a whole?

1762
02:29:59,936 --> 02:30:02,336
We have this concept of total cost of ownership.

1763
02:30:02,656 --> 02:30:06,096
this was absolutely hit on several times in my exam.

1764
02:30:06,256 --> 02:30:16,736
And this is the idea of when we measure return on investment, we can't just measure how much time it saves us or how much money it grows or how much risk it reduced or how much cost it saved.

1765
02:30:16,976 --> 02:30:20,736
In order to get a return on investment, return on investment is a two-part equation.

1766
02:30:20,816 --> 02:30:26,176
It's what increased the increase in revenue, so to speak, as well as over the increase in expenses.

1767
02:30:26,336 --> 02:30:32,096
If we increase the revenue by 5%, but we increase our expenses by 10%, we have a negative return on investment.

1768
02:30:32,238 --> 02:30:40,656
And so it's this idea of how do we understand how much this thing is costing us so we can compare it to how much this thing this thing is helping us to find a adequate return on investment.

1769
02:30:40,750 --> 02:30:49,668
And the TCO or total cost of investment is really this idea of what all goes into this, because it's not just your co-pilot credits or your tokens.

1770
02:30:49,856 --> 02:30:56,336
We need to make sure we consider development costs, we need to make sure we consider deployment costs, we need to make sure we consider operational costs.

1771
02:30:56,526 --> 02:30:57,936
You know, we talked about an operational model.

1772
02:30:58,126 --> 02:31:05,936
If we don't have have to hire four new people to monitor our agents, the cost of those four new people should be considered in our total cost of ownership.

1773
02:31:06,096 --> 02:31:07,940
Data quality and preparation costs, right?

1774
02:31:08,336 --> 02:31:11,216
We talk all about making sure everything is prepared and ready for agents.

1775
02:31:11,376 --> 02:31:15,776
What kind of time and effort and people were required and skills do we have to acquire in order to do that?

1776
02:31:15,936 --> 02:31:19,456
The cost of those things should be calculated in our total cost of ownership.

1777
02:31:19,616 --> 02:31:20,656
Change management costs.

1778
02:31:20,816 --> 02:31:31,456
So not only the time spent from, you know, say we had a two hour training with 10 people, not just how much it cost to pay 10 people for two hours of work, but also how much time did the trainer have to spend?

1779
02:31:31,616 --> 02:31:36,736
What kind of resources were spent to, you know, to execute maybe the hands-on training?

1780
02:31:36,896 --> 02:31:40,096
What kind of effort was put into adoption communication, right?

1781
02:31:40,256 --> 02:31:42,576
In terms of keeping documentation up to date, right?

1782
02:31:42,656 --> 02:31:47,296
Oh, well, somebody has to spend a couple hours every week reviewing agent documents and making sure they're up to date, right?

1783
02:31:47,598 --> 02:31:49,616
Like those are all things that go on the total cost of ownership.

1784
02:31:49,710 --> 02:31:51,616
and we also have decommissioned costs as well.

1785
02:31:51,776 --> 02:31:58,656
A lot of people like to just think of operational costs in terms of like, oh, our agent used this many copilot credits, but it saved us this much time.

1786
02:31:58,736 --> 02:31:59,456
Like we're good to go.

1787
